<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Brown (Canadian politician)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1818–1880</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Brown was a Scottish-born Canadian journalist and politician who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> Globe and led the Reformers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> West. A champion of representation by population, he joined his rivals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald">John A. Macdonald</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-etienne-cartier">George-Étienne Cartier</a> in the Great Coalition of 1864, which opened the way to Confederation. A Father of Confederation, he died in 1880 after being shot by a dismissed employee.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-brown-canadian-politician.jpg" alt="George Brown (Canadian politician)" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hunter &amp;amp; Co. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Brown.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
